In this lesson we'll look at how to turn a pile of numbers into a clear answer. Once you've collected your data, there are really three steps to follow. First, organise it with a display. For a small data set, a dot plot or stem-and-leaf plot works well because you can see every value. For bigger sets or grouped continuous data, a histogram is your friend because it reveals the shape. And if you're comparing groups, try a two-way table or a bar chart. Next, analyse by computing summary statistics. Report the centre using both the mean and the median, and lean on the median if there might be outliers. For spread, give the range, or the interquartile range for grouped data. Don't forget to flag any outliers either. Finally, report your findings. A good report restates the question at the top, names where the data came from and how big the sample is, shows your display, describes the distribution in terms of shape, centre and spread, then finishes with one or two sentences that directly answer the original question. If the data can't fully answer it, say so honestly. Now let's see it in action.